    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 15th with an offensive focus on leveraging high-ceiling matchups against middling arms. The strongest play on the board is **Yordan Alvarez** (75.68), who carries an elite 0.421 xwOBA into his matchup against Jack Leiter. With a strong platoon advantage and a favorable spot in the order, **Yordan Alvarez** remains our anchor. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (70.21) also provides immense value today; despite batting ninth, his aggressive baserunning is amplified by the slow delivery of Sean Burke, creating a high probability for a stolen base\u2014our most coveted commodity. Similarly, **Junior Caminero** (67.27) and **Casey Schmitt** (63.20) are set to capitalize on favorable park factors and platoon splits, making them essential pieces of today\u2019s offensive structure.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved the benching of **Caleb Durbin** (52.35). While **Caleb Durbin** possesses the speed profile we prioritize, his projected floor was suppressed by a lower lineup position and a challenging matchup against Strider. The optimizer ultimately opted for the higher situational efficiency of **Christian Yelich** (60.32), who benefits from a projected wind-out scenario and a solid platoon edge against Joe Ryan, and **Luke Raley** (59.77), whose high SP-skill advantage against Randy V\u00e1squez outweighed the utility of keeping **Caleb Durbin** active. \n\nWe are monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for several key contributors; should **Trea Turner** drop in the order or if weather shifts impact the wind-out projections for **Gary Sanchez**, we will need to re-evaluate our pinch-hit risk. Furthermore, while **Hyeseong Kim** (59.91) provides utility, fantasy managers should note that his projection is slightly tempered by a high-efficiency catcher behind the plate, which introduces a moderate SB risk. Finally, ensure **Zac Veen** remains benched as he continues his stint with the Albuquerque Isotopes; he is currently unavailable for active duty.",
